- I'll assign: SHOULD for the whole, or split: SHOULD for "every change adds a line" (repeat of git, unmeasured value — dave), MUST for "earlier lines are never edited" (append-only log integrity; a tampered log is a provenance failure). Is that irreversible? Not fully (git recovers). Hmm. But append-only is the audit invariant; editing an earlier line is the one edit that changes the record's meaning while looking like it was always so. That's a silent corruption of provenance. I'd call it a safety bound for the record → MUST? Let me weigh with the "does it destroy/recover" test: git can reveal the previous line in history, so it's recoverable with effort. I'll assign SHOULD for both, but note "earlier lines never edited" is the stronger and is a promotion candidate, because the whole point of `changed:` is a cheap visible trail. Round 1 sun-tzu said flat SHOULD. Keep SHOULD. Actually, let me reconsider: the Fails-if for W4.2 is "git shows a change to an entry with no matching changed: line" — that's about missing additions, not edited earlier lines. So editing earlier lines isn't even in the Fails-if. SHOULD is right. Keep SHOULD.

Hmm, but I want to be decisive and not too wishy-washy. I'll say SHOULD, one line.

W5.1: "Written immediately: a decision or fact, before the code; a human correction; and the state of unfinished work, before a handoff/interruption/context reduction."
Round 1 sun-tzu: SHOULD.
- "state of unfinished work before a handoff/interruption/context reduction": context that ends with the session takes the state with it; nothing else holds it. Irreversible loss (K5.2 mitigates: the journal covers it — the brief/K5.2 says a session cut off before reading loses nothing because the next read covers it). Hmm, K5.2 says journal reading covers lost sessions. But the *state* note (what I was doing) is not in the journal necessarily. Lao-tzu says MUST for the handoff state. Sun Tzu #17 (learn by probing/signs) and #2 (readiness). Is there a gate? Fails-if: "For a human correction, it fails if the session ends without one (judged)." The unfinished-work-before-handoff is not in the Fails-if explicitly, though W5.1's Fails-if mentions committed code without an entry. So no gate for the handoff state.
- I'll split: SHOULD for "decision/fact before the code" (ordering; K1.1 is the binding gate) and SHOULD for "state of unfinished work before a handoff" — but that one is a loss bound. Ugh. Let me assign MUST to the handoff state? Round 1 I said SHOULD. Lao-tzu said MUST. The irreversible harm: if a session dies, the next agent doesn't know what was in flight; but K5.2 says the next read covers the journal. The state.md is the mechanism for carrying on (Rd6). If it's missing, the handoff fails; Rd6 is the test. It's judged. I'll keep SHOULD for the whole W5.1 but note the handoff-state clause is the strongest and would be MUST if a check is built. Actually, consistency: I just assigned W3.5 MUST for irreversible leak even without a check. Here the harm is also irreversible (lost state). Why SHOULD? Because K5.2 provides a backstop (the journal) — so it's not irrecoverable. That's a good distinction: the state is recoverable from the journal/next read, so SHOULD. I'll say that. Keep SHOULD.

W5.3: "done: added when work complete: time, result, test results or 'no tests'. Points at evidence. Standing rule never done. Mismatch → Rd5.3."
Round 1 sun-tzu: SHOULD.
- "done:" = completion bookkeeping; evidence exists in runs/ and commits; a reader/reviewer checks. SHOULD.
- "a mismatch is reported, not settled in done:" = that's Rd5.3's gate. MUST? It's already Rd5.3. In W5.3's row, dave says MUST for the mismatch part. But it's a pointer. I could split: SHOULD for `done:`; MUST for "a mismatch is reported, not settled" (but that's Rd5.3's rule, so better to say it belongs to Rd5.3 and give it MUST there). I'll assign SHOULD to W5.3 and note the mismatch clause is MUST under Rd5.3. Actually the requirement W5.3 itself contains "it is reported, not settled in done:". I'll split: MUST for that clause (irreversible: settling a mismatch overwrites the record of divergence — a false record) — hmm. Let me assign: SHOULD for adding `done:`; MUST for "if the result differs, it is reported and not settled in done:" because settling it silently destroys the divergence (nothing later shows it; that's exactly the silent loss) → MUST. Yes, matches dave.

W6.1: "Each new entry its own file. Agents never append to one shared file; each journal one file per agent session."
Round 1 sun-tzu: SHOULD.
- Collisions: two writers appending to one file → lost updates; one changed: line can't name both authors (lao-tzu). Irreversible? Git can often recover from a merge, but a silent overwrite loses it. W6.3's Fails-if covers "an edit is lost without a conflict." With one file per entry, conflicts are visible. The no-shared-file rule is a structural defense; it's the mechanism that makes W6.3 work. Is it a safety bound or a method? It's the structure that prevents silent loss. Fan-li/lao-tzu MUST. Dave SHOULD (serves W6.3). I'll say SHOULD — because it's a method serving the no-loss outcome, and if the outcome (W6.3) is the MUST, the file-per-entry convention is the default means. Also one-file-per-session for journals is a method. Keep SHOULD. Note: if W6.3 is MUST, W6.1 is its chosen mechanism; overriding W6.1 would require another way to guarantee no silent loss. That's a good reason to keep SHOULD.

W6.3: "A claim is never rewritten, so a merge conflict is only in added lines. Whoever merges keeps both sides: both changed: lines, both links. Nobody chooses between two claims. Two entries superseding the same entry reported in state.md and to EJ (Rd5.3). INDEX conflict resolved by regenerating (K6.3)."
Round 1 sun-tzu: MUST.
- Merge dropping one side loses data silently → MUST. The regenerating INDEX clause is method → SHOULD (K6.3/K6.1). The reporting clauses defer to Rd5.3 → MUST as part of the no-loss/deferral. Keep W6.3 MUST (for "keeps both sides; nobody chooses"). Split off "regenerate INDEX" as SHOULD. Also note the K4.3/W6.3 conflict: K4.3 lists what an entry may gain (changed:, status, superseded_by, done:), while W6.3 says a merge keeps "both links". If a second superseding entry adds a `supersedes` link on the old entry? Hmm, the conflict: K4.3 against W6.3 — K4.3 says an entry after first commit only gains changed:, new status, superseded_by, done:. W6.3 says a merge keeps "both links". Which links? If a conflict in an entry is in the lines added to it, and W6.3 says keep both links, but K4.3 doesn't list a link that could be added... Actually W6.3's "both links" likely refers to links a side added (e.g., superseded_by, or a link from a related entry). The conflict EJ must settle. I can say my level on the "both links" clause depends on that settlement. Good — the brief invites me to say how my level depends.

W6.3 final: MUST for "nobody chooses between two claims; the merge keeps both sides"; SHOULD for "regenerate INDEX.md" (method, gate is K6.2/K6.3); the "both links" clause: MUST if K4.3 is read to permit link additions, otherwise it needs EJ's settlement (I'll note dependency).

K1.1: "To change behaviour that a decision or fact governs, change the entry first, then the code. Work that has to go the other way is a matter for the project's workflow, not for memory."
Round 1 sun-tzu: MUST.
- Changing code before the entry turns the record into a description of the code and loses the reason; git cannot restore the reason. Irreversible loss of rationale → MUST? But lao-tzu and dave say SHOULD: it imposes an order of work (RFC §6 warns against imposing method), and Rd5 catches drift. Hmm. This is the crux. Sun Tzu #1: reckon before battle — write the decision before committing to the action. #2: make yourself unbeatable first; win then seek battle — decide first, then act. That maps strongly to entry-first! Sun Tzu's whole first rule is "decide (count) before you commit." And #5: move only on advantage. I think entry-first is the decision-before-action rule, which is central to Sun Tzu. But is it a "gate that stops work"? K1.1's Fails-if is judged and the design removed K2/K3. The reason it's not machine-gated is that "behaviour a decision governs" is semantic. Still, the loss of the reason is irreversible: git can show what changed but not why, if the entry was written after. That's lao-tzu's exact point. Under my test (a) irreversible loss of the reason → MUST. I'll keep MUST, and say what moved me (nothing; I keep it) and note the check is judged and absent, so the gate must be built or it holds only by the writer's discipline. Hmm, but the four split: linus SHOULD, fan-li MUST, sun-tzu MUST, lao-tzu SHOULD, dave SHOULD. My keeping MUST makes it 3 SHOULD vs 2 MUST. Fine; round 2 is individual final levels.

Actually, let me reconsider carefully, because Sun Tzu's #1/#2 are about *reckoning* before committing, not necessarily about the artifact order. The entry is the recorded decision; writing code first then recording the decision means the "decision" was really a rationalization. Sun Tzu #6: deliberate benefit and harm before committing. #17: probe then read signs. Yes, decide before acting is core. MUST.

But hold on — K1.1 says "Work that has to go the other way is a matter for the project's workflow, not for memory." That's actually a release valve: if you must code first, that's a workflow matter. So K1.1 already carves out its own override, making it arguably a default, not an absolute gate. Hmm. If the rule itself names a legitimate override path (project workflow), then by the level definitions it's a default → SHOULD. That's a strong textual argument for SHOULD. Dave and lao-tzu say SHOULD; dave cites RFC §6.

Let me think about which is more Sun-Tzu. Sun Tzu #13: "Not every road is taken, and not every command of the ruler is accepted" — discretion is a rule, and it's MUST-worthy. But discretion means the *rule* allows exceptions; that's exactly what SHOULD is (overridable with reason). So a rule with an explicit override path is SHOULD. K1.1 has an explicit override path ("work that has to go the other way is a matter for the project's workflow"). Therefore K1.1 is a default → SHOULD. That's a clean, principled ruling, and it changes my round-1 MUST. I'll change to SHOULD, moved by the clause's own release valve and RFC §6, and note the loss of the reason is real (my #6) — which is why I'd want the workflow to record the override.

Hmm, but wait: the override path is "a matter for the project's workflow" — meaning the workflow decides, not that any individual may ignore it. And K1.1's loss (the reason) is irreversible. I'm torn. Let me decide based on the framing of the levels: MUST = "the commit/task/handoff does not go ahead" when it fails. For K1.1, the failure is "git shows a commit changing governed behaviour with no change to the entry in the same or an earlier commit (judged)." That IS a gate on the commit — if judged. The parenthetical (judged) means a reviewer decides; no reviewer today. Hmm.

I think I'll change K1.1 to SHOULD. Reasons: (1) RFC §6 — entry-first orders the work, a method; (2) the rule itself names a workflow override; (3) the loss (the reason) is partly recoverable because the mind that wrote the code is still around at the time and can write the entry after, and Rd5/the entry-before-commit requirement catches the drift at review. (4) Sun Tzu #8: speed — adding a strict order gate to every code change is a heavy method. But #1/#2 argue the other way. Hmm.

Actually, let me look at the Fails-if text: "**Fails if** git shows a commit changing the behaviour a decision or fact governs, with no change to that entry in the same commit or an earlier one (judged)." Notice: "in the same commit or an earlier one." So you can write the entry in the same commit, not necessarily before. That weakens "entry first" substantially; it's really "entry in the same commit as the code." So the gate is about not letting code land without its entry — which is a real, checkable-by-a-reviewer bound (git shows it). And it's about the reason being recorded, even if written in the same commit. So K1.1 as enforced is "no code without its entry" (MUST-ish), while "entry before code" (the literal ordering) is method. I'll split: MUST for "no commit changes governed behaviour without the entry in the same or an earlier commit" (that's the actual Fails-if, a checkable bound on the commit, protecting the reason); SHOULD for "change the entry first, then the code" (ordering; workflow may reverse it). That's precise and defensible, and it lets me keep my round-1 MUST on the operative half while honoring RFC §6. I like this. Changed from round 1: yes, split (I had flat MUST).

Hmm, but is the "no code without entry" a MUST if judged and no reviewer? The Fails-if says judged. No reviewer → not in force. Ugh, the same issue. I'll state the level as MUST (safety bound: rationale is irreplaceable) and flag the missing reviewer. Or SHOULD. Let me set a consistent policy and apply it to all: **A MUST is a bound whose failure is silent and unrecoverable; a missing checker is a build item, not a reason to downgrade.** But then W3.4... is it silent/unrecoverable? A false "verified" claim can be checked by a reader against code; it's not silent, and a new entry corrects it. So SHOULD. W1.3 one-claim: silent and unrecoverable (can't separate later) → MUST. W2.2 mislabelled guess: not silent (the claim is still there to check) and correctable → SHOULD. W3.5 private memory leak: silent and unrecoverable → MUST. K1.1 missing entry: silent (the reason never existed) and unrecoverable → MUST for "no code without its entry"; SHOULD for ordering. Good, this policy is consistent.

Let me re-examine W4.2 "earlier lines never edited": editing a changed: line — silent? partly; unrecoverable? git history has the old line, so recoverable. → SHOULD. Good.
W5.1 handoff state: recoverable via journal/K5.2 → SHOULD. Good.
