Rd2.1: "At session start, an agent reads AGENTS.md, INDEX.md, the work/ listing and its task's state.md. Loaded wherever the tool allows... Only where none exists does the first instruction ask."
Round 1 sun-tzu: SHOULD. Linus: mixed MUST/SHOULD. Fan-li/lao-tzu: MUST. Dave: SHOULD.
- Reading the four files at session start = orientation; a small task may override with a written reason. Is the harm irreversible? Work begun before the files arrive is work on memory nobody read; but it's correctable. The Fails-if: "a session starts work on a task before the four in Rd2.1 have reached it (judged, from the start of the session)." That's a gate on starting work. Hmm. I'll split: MUST for "receive governing instructions (AGENTS.md) and task state (state.md) before starting work" — a bound: acting without the governing instructions is acting on unknown constraints (#1 reckon before battle; #17 read the signs; #16 orders clear first). SHOULD for the four-file startup recipe and the loading mechanisms (CLI-specific method; a small task may override). That matches linus's split and is Sun-Tzu-consistent: you must reckon/read before acting, but the recipe is method. Round 1 I said SHOULD flat; change to split. Reason: the harm is acting against unknown governing instructions; the mechanism is method.

Hmm, is "AGENTS.md and state.md before starting" silent+unrecoverable? Acting on stale state could produce a wrong commit; but it's caught later. It's a readiness rule (#2). I'll call the "read governing instructions and task state before acting" MUST (readiness), and the specific four-file ritual SHOULD. Good.

Rd2.3: "Never loaded, at session start or later: superseded/retired entries (except by link); the whole project-memory tree at once; whole graph/code-map dumps; bulk issue/PR text; another agent's transcripts; credentials."
Round 1 sun-tzu: SHOULD. Dave: MUST credentials, SHOULD other five. Lao-tzu: MUST.
- credentials: MUST (safety bound; matches W3.2). 
- another agent's transcripts: ties to W3.3 (private state) → MUST? The brief's W3.3 private state is a confidentiality bound. In Rd2.3 the prohibition on loading another agent's transcripts is also confidentiality + context. I'd say MUST for credentials and another agent's transcripts (privacy/safety), SHOULD for the context-budget items (whole tree, code-map dumps, bulk PR text, superseded entries). Hmm, but "another agent's transcripts" — if it's the same as W3.3's private state, then MUST. But transcripts vs private state: W3.3 says "No raw transcripts, and nothing from another agent's private state" — I gave transcripts SHOULD and private state MUST. In Rd2.3, "another agent's transcripts" is the loading side of the same transcript item → SHOULD (consistent with W3.3). And "credentials" MUST. So Rd2.3: MUST for credentials (and arguably private state, but it says transcripts); SHOULD for the other five. That matches dave and keeps consistency. Round 1 SHOULD flat; change to split (credentials MUST). 

Wait, does Rd2.3 include another agent's private state? The verbatim list: "superseded or retired entries, except by following a link (Rd4.4); the whole project-memory/ tree at once; whole graph or code-map dumps; bulk issue or PR text; another agent's transcripts; credentials." So six items: (1) superseded/retired entries, (2) whole tree, (3) graph/code-map dumps, (4) bulk issue/PR text, (5) another agent's transcripts, (6) credentials. Dave said MUST credentials, SHOULD other five. I'll do the same, but consider (5) another agent's transcripts: W3.3 gave raw transcripts SHOULD (volume/provenance), private state MUST. Another agent's transcripts here = raw transcripts → SHOULD. OK. And (1) superseded/retired entries — reading them without a link can mislead; but it's a context default → SHOULD. Fine. So Rd2.3: MUST for credentials; SHOULD for the other five. Changed from round 1 (flat SHOULD) → now split credentials MUST.

Rd3.1: "INDEX.md fits the strictest start-file limit among agents. The K6 check also fails when it doesn't fit. Example: 24 KB / 200 lines."
Round 1 sun-tzu: "MUST if the K6 check tests the size limit; SHOULD otherwise"; dave says MUST (tool checks). COO fact: memory_index.py fails on "an INDEX.md ... over its size limits." So the check tests size → MUST. Keep: MUST. The example numbers are guidance (not measurement) — I'll note that; the figure is guidance, the bound is MUST. Changed? Refine: now that the tool tests the limit, it is unconditionally MUST; the numbers are guidance. Round 1 said conditional; now resolved to MUST.

Rd4.2: "It searches entries/ by text: grep, or the full-text cache once its trigger fires."
Round 1 sun-tzu: SHOULD. Fan-li/lao-tzu/dave: guidance. Linus: SHOULD.
- How to search = method; nothing fails if you use a third way. That's guidance by the level definition ("explanation or advice; nothing fails when it is not followed"). Hmm. My round 1 said SHOULD ("How to search is method"). But a method that is a default you may override with a reason is SHOULD; pure advice is guidance. Is using grep a default? It's a recommendation; any effective search works. The requirement says "It searches entries/ by text: grep, or the cache." Nothing fails if you use a different text search. So guidance. But the important part is "search the entries by text" (as opposed to guessing) — that's a default that matters. I'll say: SHOULD for "search entries/ by text before relying on memory" (a default that supports Rd4.1/Rd5); guidance for "grep or the cache" (choice of tool). Hmm, that's a split. Actually the requirement is only about the mechanism. The "search by text" is the operative default. I'll assign SHOULD (mechanism default; a third way is fine, so it's a weak SHOULD) or guidance. Let me pick guidance for the specific mechanism and say the default is "search the entries" which lives in Rd4.1/Rd4.2. Hmm.

Let me keep it simple and consistent with my round 1: SHOULD. Reason: "search the entries by text" is a default retrieval discipline that a task can override (e.g., when `covers` already points to the entry); the particular tool (grep/cache) is guidance. Actually, I realize I'm overcomplicating. Dave/fan-li/lao-tzu say guidance; linus and I say SHOULD. I'll change to **guidance** for the mechanism, because nothing fails when a third search method is used, and say the operative default (search before relying) is Rd4.1/Rd5.1. Changed from round 1: yes, SHOULD→guidance, moved by the level definition (nothing fails) — and by #8 (don't inflate a tool choice into a default). Hmm, but is there value in marking it SHOULD? A SHOULD is a default with a written reason to override. Choosing a different search method hardly needs a written reason. So guidance is right. I'll change. Good.

Rd4.5: "An agent may read another agent's memory. It treats it as that agent's memory, not project memory, and says whose it is: the agent name and the tool."
Round 1 sun-tzu: SHOULD.
- Naming provenance = labelling default. Is the harm irreversible? If you read a private agent rule as project authority, you may act wrongly; but a reader can correct, and a new entry can fix. So SHOULD. Keep SHOULD. Maybe split: SHOULD for "treat as that agent's memory, name the agent and tool"; the permission ("may read") is guidance (removing a barrier). I'll say guidance for the permission, SHOULD for attribution. Round 1 flat SHOULD → refine to split. Fine.

